---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import { buildPersonSchema, buildProfessionalServiceSchema } from '../lib/schema.js';

// Copy source: Workspace/04-Marketing/Website/homepage-copy-DRAFT-2026-08-15.md
// (internal draft, 2026-08-15). Reproduced faithfully; wording is not yet
// Jonathan-approved. No public pricing, no client name, no measured ROI claim.

const personSchema = buildPersonSchema({
  description: 'Jonathan Malkin maps the knowledge trapped in an owner-led business, in people, tools, and scattered files, onto one page, then builds the company brain the team and their AI can actually use.',
  url: 'https://builtwithjon.com',
  knowsAbout: [
    'company brain systems',
    'business knowledge mapping',
    'AI-ready operations',
    'AI workshops in Austin',
  ],
});

const websiteSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebSite',
  name: 'Jonathan Malkin',
  url: 'https://builtwithjon.com',
  description: 'Start with the Business Map: put your people, process, and systems on one page, then get a clear decision about what to build first.',
};

const professionalServiceSchema = buildProfessionalServiceSchema({
  description: 'Business knowledge mapping and company brain builds for owner-led businesses in Austin and across the United States.',
  founder: personSchema,
  makesOffer: [
    {
      '@type': 'Offer',
      name: 'The Business Map',
      description: 'The first phase of a build: your people, process, and systems mapped onto one page, then a clear decision about what to build first.',
      url: 'https://builtwithjon.com/business-map/',
    },
  ],
});
---

<Base
  title="Company Brain Systems for Owner-Led Businesses"
  description="Jonathan Malkin maps the knowledge trapped in your people and tools onto one page, then builds the company brain your team and AI can actually use. Start with the Business Map."
  schema={personSchema}
>
  <JsonLd schema={websiteSchema} />
  <JsonLd schema={professionalServiceSchema} />

  <section class="hero-split container-wide">
    <div class="hero-split-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
      <h1>You can't see where the money goes.</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        The answers live in people's heads. Everything routes back through you. Frantic, and never caught up.
      </p>
      <p class="subtitle">
        There's a project you keep putting off because pulling the information together is too painful.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-links">
        <a href="/business-map/#start" class="hero-btn">Start with the Business Map</a>
      </div>
      <p class="subtitle">
        I take one build at a time. The next Business Map can start as the current build wraps.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-split-media">
      <img
        src="/home/workshop-room.jpg"
        width="1600"
        height="900"
        alt="A full room of people watching Jonathan present a live AI demo on screen at AI Tinkerers in Sao Paulo."
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Live demo at AI Tinkerers, Sao Paulo</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="container section-band" aria-labelledby="cost-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">What flying blind costs</p>
    <h2 id="cost-h">What is flying blind actually costing you?</h2>
    <div class="proof-strip">
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>You can't see your own business.</strong>
        Money moves through the business and you can't say exactly where. Decisions get made on guesswork instead of what actually happened. Your team knows things you don't, and you find out after it matters.
      </div>
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>There's the project you keep avoiding.</strong>
        It's the thing you actually want to do next, the one you can't start because getting the information together is too painful. It sits there. You route around it.
      </div>
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>And there's what happens if one person leaves.</strong>
        The knowledge that keeps the business running lives in a handful of heads. If one of them walks, so does the answer.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section-band" aria-labelledby="get-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">What you get</p>
    <h2 id="get-h">What do you actually get?</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      It's folders and files. Your team can read them. So can your AI assistants. That means real help with real work, not generic advice. Nothing about it locks you into a platform. High-stakes decisions stay with people, not automation.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">I call the finished system a company brain.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section-band" aria-labelledby="playbook-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why the usual playbook fails here</p>
    <h2 id="playbook-h">Why doesn't the usual playbook work here?</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      In a big company, ten people do one job, so you automate a slice and free eight. In a small business nobody does just one thing, so the enterprise playbook falls apart.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section-band" aria-labelledby="proof-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof, not theory</p>
    <h2 id="proof-h">Has this actually worked?</h2>
    <div class="proof-strip">
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>~5,000 to ~900</strong>
        One client's business had about 5,000 scattered messages. Working through them narrowed that to about 1,200 in-scope threads, then to about 900 organized, privacy-treated files the team and their AI use.
      </div>
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>696 artifacts</strong>
        That same build organized 696 content artifacts into one system.
      </div>
      <div class="proof-item">
        <strong>770 checks, all green</strong>
        It shipped against 770 automated checks.
      </div>
    </div>
    <blockquote>
      &ldquo;It's just all over. I open up about fifteen different things every month and export spreadsheets and try to get them all into one.&rdquo;
    </blockquote>
    <div class="panel" style="padding: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 1rem;">
      <p style="margin: 0;">
        A lot of the source material was sensitive, so every human-sourced item went through privacy treatment with a required human review step. That's treatment, not a guarantee of complete de-identification. It was backed by a targeted primary-source legal issue scan, which is a scan, not a legal opinion.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>The client owns the platform, the credentials, the data, and the final system.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section-band" aria-labelledby="after-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">What comes next</p>
    <h2 id="after-h">What happens after the Business Map?</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      We start with the Business Map: your people, process, and systems on one page, plus a clear decision about what to build first. It's the first phase of a build, not a standalone report, and it ends in a straightforward call: continue or stop.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      If it's a go, the build happens in phases and you own everything as it's built: the platform, the credentials, the data.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Once the system is running, ongoing support keeps it current. Regular working meetings, not a mailbox you wait on.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Down the road, the same brain can extend into a relationship system, the kind that preps meetings and drafts follow-ups for you to approve. That's an extension, not the starting point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container why-me-grid section-band">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Who's behind this</p>
      <h2>Who's behind this?</h2>
      <p class="cred-line">
        20+ years in enterprise tech, 8 of them at Automation Anywhere, where I scaled a global SE org from 6 people to 250. I'm based in Austin. I meet clients in Austin rooms and deliver anywhere.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/about/">More about how I work &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <figure class="why-me-media">
      <img
        src="/home/jules-demo.jpg"
        width="1400"
        height="787"
        alt="Close view of a live AI assistant demo on screen, showing the presenter alongside a real-time AI avatar."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="container home-final-cta">
    <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
    <h2>Start with the Business Map</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      I take one build at a time. The next Business Map can start as the current build wraps.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-links" style="justify-content: center;">
      <a href="/business-map/#start" class="hero-btn">Start with the Business Map</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
